window.REVIEW_DETAILS["ny_c-15968b8295d97f81"]={"key":"ny_c-15968b8295d97f81","group":"ny_c","uid":"15968b8295d97f81","rank":49,"title":"willowmere tanneryside reit tender decision","occupation":"personal_financial_advisors","archetype":"authored_doc","subbatch":"corpus_c1","prompt":"You’re Jelena Coutinho, the personal financial advisor at Willowmere Solutions responsible for preparing the client case response that Edith Lombardi in legal will approve before Clara Bruns is called. Clara is 68 and owns 41,000 units in a non-traded real estate investment program sponsored by Tanneryside Trading Company. She acquired the entire position years ago through a broker who is no longer available, her recorded aggregate cost basis is $502,250, and the position is not held in a retirement account. The sponsor’s tender window closes at 5:00 p.m. on 30 September 2026, so Edith needs a decision-ready record rather than another holding summary. Clara has said that she wants the best value available, but she also needs at least $280,000 of cleared proceeds by 15 October 2026 for a care-community deposit and does not want more units sold than are necessary. Maude Dalgaard has confirmed that the deposit date is firm. Farida Gallagher circulated an earlier internal note describing the sponsor’s stated net asset value as what Clara’s units are worth today; Edith will not approve a client response that leaves that impression uncorrected. There are no attachments and no outside research should be added to the case record; use only the facts in this message, identify any decision-relevant unknown rather than filling it with an assumption, and do not state an exact tax result, future distribution, or future unit value. Tanneryside currently displays a stated NAV of $10.42 per unit, or $427,220 for Clara’s position. The underlying appraisal date is 31 December 2023, the NAV was posted on 28 March 2024, and it has not subsequently been refreshed. The sponsor suspended its ordinary redemption plan on 14 November 2024 and has not announced a resumption, so holding the units provides no scheduled liquidity before Clara’s deposit deadline. The sponsor’s present tender is $7.60 per unit, permits tenders of any number of units, charges a 2% processing fee against accepted proceeds, may be prorated if aggregate tenders exceed the 5,000,000-unit program limit, and is expected to settle on 15 November 2026. Acceptance and the accepted unit count are not guaranteed. Clara can withdraw her tender until the deadline, but the sponsor has not offered accelerated settlement. The record also contains four expired unsolicited mini-tenders: $6.35 per unit for up to 25,000 units expiring 31 January 2025, $6.70 for up to 60,000 units expiring 31 March 2025, $7.15 for up to 35,000 units expiring 30 June 2025, and $7.40 for up to 50,000 units expiring 31 August 2025. None remains exercisable, and none supplies evidence that Clara herself completed a sale. Actual secondary-platform transactions reported for this program were 4,000 units at $6.20 and 2,500 at $6.35 in the fourth quarter of 2024; 5,000 at $6.55 and 3,000 at $6.70 in the first quarter of 2025; 2,000 at $6.85, 7,500 at $7.00, and 4,000 at $7.10 in the second quarter of 2025; and 1,500 at $7.20 and 6,000 at $7.05 in the third quarter of 2025. These are completed trades rather than listings, and the secondary platform charges sellers 1% of gross proceeds. The current executable secondary bid available to Clara is $7.05 per unit for all 41,000 units, with no financing condition, a 1% seller fee, settlement on 20 September 2026, and expiry at 5:00 p.m. on 12 September 2026. The buyer will not purchase a partial position. No other current secondary bid is documented. Clara’s request not to sell excess units therefore conflicts with the only documented secondary transaction that can clear enough cash by her deadline, while the higher sponsor tender offers more nominal proceeds but cannot provide cleared funds by that date and may not accept every unit. Willowmere policy allows you to make a bounded recommendation among tendering, holding, or accepting the documented secondary bid, but you may not recommend an outcome that depends on an undocumented buyer, assume that a conditional tender will be accepted in full, or present an expired bid as currently available. Prepare a concise client case response for Edith that establishes a defensible valuation range, compares the evidence across periods, explains the material changes and the difference between NAV and realizable evidence, shows the economics of the relevant alternatives, resolves Clara’s competing requirements on the record, and makes exactly one recommendation. The recommendation must be anchored to a single expected net-proceeds figure that agrees everywhere it appears, and the response must finish on the decision itself rather than on a list of next steps. Keep it to no more than four pages, make it suitable for legal review and a subsequent client conversation, and write only `clara_bruns_reit_decision.pdf` into the top level of the working directory using exactly that filename.\n\n---\n\nWrite your finished work to the **top level of the current working directory**, using exactly these filenames:\n\n- `clara_bruns_reit_decision.pdf` — A client case response presenting the valuation range, period comparison, alternative economics, and one tender-or-sale decision for legal review. (Use only the closed-record facts in the request; no more than four pages; include exactly one recommendation with a consistent net-proceeds anchor; finish on the decision; do not invent figures or state an exact tax result.)\n\nNothing outside those filenames is collected. A correct file written anywhere else, or under any other name, scores zero.\n","inputs":[],"gold":[{"name":"package/private/gold/clara_bruns_reit_decision.pdf","bytes":11950,"extension":".pdf","url":"assets/987f356528b0c90c504d7d6e52651c3019d3183cbed06f98b2cee74f83a2f266.pdf","sha256":"987f356528b0c90c504d7d6e52651c3019d3183cbed06f98b2cee74f83a2f266","preview":{"kind":"pdf","text":"WILLOWMERE SOLUTIONS / CONFIDENTIAL CLIENT CASE RESPONSE\n\n\n\n DECISION RECORD | 30 AUGUST 2026\n\n\n              Clara Bruns — Tanneryside Trading Company liquidity decision\n Prepared by Jelena Coutinho, Personal Financial Advisor, for Edith Lombardi / Legal. Closed-record analysis for a firm care-community deposit of $280,000 in cleared funds by 15\n October 2026.\n\n  Position                                     Recorded basis                       Hard requirement                                 Decision set\n\n  41,000 units of the non-traded Tanneryside   $502,250 aggregate recorded          Deposit date is firm; Maude Dalgaard             Bounded policy choice: sponsor\n  Trading Company real estate investment       cost basis ($12.25/unit); not held   confirmed it. Clara seeks best available         tender, hold, or documented\n  program                                      in a retirement account              value and prefers not to sell more units than    secondary bid. No undocumented\n                                                                                    necessary.                                       buyer or partial buyer may be\n                                                                                                                                     assumed.\n\n\n Decision lens: value evidence is not the same as available cash\n Tanneryside displays NAV of $10.42/unit ($427,220). That figure is appraisal-based: underlying appraisal dated 31 December 2023, posted 28 March 2024, with no subsequent refresh. It must\n not be presented as Clara’s current cash-equivalent value, guaranteed sale price, or directly realizable market value. It is a sponsor-reported estimate, not a current observable public-market price.\n\n  Present evidence                               Gross per unit / position            What it means for valuation\n\n  Documented executable secondary bid            $7.05 / $289,050                     Lower realizability anchor: buyer will take all 41,000 units on stated terms.\n\n  Sponsor tender — conditional                   $7.60 / $311,600                     Upper realizability anchor only if accepted; timing and accepted quantity are\n                                                                                      uncertain.\n\n  Decision-oriented present gross range          $7.05–$7.60 /                        Not an average of NAV and market evidence. The $7.60 endpoint is conditional\n                                                 $289,050–$311,600                    and untimely for Clara’s need.\n\n Why the NAV gap is not resolved by “splitting the difference.” The $7.05 bid is 32.34% below NAV (discount measured against NAV). The gap can reflect the old appraisal date and appraisal\n method, non-traded illiquidity, small and irregular historical trade sizes, seller fees, and the conditions attached to actual execution. Completed trades and executable offers are realizability\n evidence; neither they nor NAV are a perfectly observable public-market price.\n\n\n\n\n                                                                                                                                                                                Clara Bruns | Legal review | 1\n\fWILLOWMERE SOLUTIONS / CONFIDENTIAL CLIENT CASE RESPONSE\n\n\n\n EVIDENCE THROUGH TIME\n\n\n                                                                      Historical market record\n Historical observations inform context only. They do not create an available offer or establish what a 41,000-unit position will clear at today.\n\n Completed secondary-platform transactions — actual clearing events, not listings, bids, or NAV\n  Period                Completed transactions                                                                   Like-for-like range / reading\n\n  Q4 2024               4,000 units @ $6.20; 2,500 @ $6.35                                                       $6.20–$6.35\n\n  Q1 2025               5,000 @ $6.55; 3,000 @ $6.70                                                             $6.55–$6.70\n\n  Q2 2025               2,000 @ $6.85; 7,500 @ $7.00; 4,000 @ $7.10                                              $6.85–$7.10\n\n  Q3 2025               1,500 @ $7.20; 6,000 @ $7.05                                                             $7.05–$7.20\n\n Like-for-like completed-trade evidence moved upward from $6.20–$6.35 in Q4 2024 to $7.05–$7.20 in Q3 2025. The direction is favorable, but the sample is small and irregular, and prices are\n dispersed. The historical prints range from 1,500 to 7,500 units; they do not guarantee continued appreciation, a current $7.20 price, or execution for all 41,000 units.\n\n Expired unsolicited mini-tenders — historical bidder appetite only; none is exercisable and none evidences a sale by Clara\n  Expired offer price              Maximum units             Expiry                          Treatment\n\n  $6.35/unit                       25,000                    31 January 2025                 Expired historical indication\n\n  $6.70/unit                       60,000                    31 March 2025                   Expired historical indication\n\n  $7.15/unit                       35,000                    30 June 2025                    Expired historical indication\n\n  $7.40/unit                       50,000                    31 August 2025                  Expired historical indication\n\n These unsolicited offers suggest changing bidder appetite over time, but they are not completed transactions, do not prove Clara sold any units, and cannot be treated as currently available prices.\n\n Liquidity context\n The ordinary redemption plan was suspended on 14 November 2024 and no resumption has been announced. Holding is not a statement that the units are worthless or permanently unsaleable; it\n means there is no scheduled liquidity before 15 October 2026 on this record.\n\n\n\n\n                                                                                                                                                                              Clara Bruns | Legal review | 2\n\fWILLOWMERE SOLUTIONS / CONFIDENTIAL CLIENT CASE RESPONSE\n\n\n\n ALTERNATIVES\n\n\n                                                                Economics and timing test\n Amounts are shown consistently as gross proceeds less stated seller/processing fee = net proceeds. “Expected” does not eliminate stated execution conditions.\n\n  Alternative                  Economics for 41,000 units                 Expiry / settlement                Execution and deadline result\n\n  Accept current secondary     Gross $289,050                             Expires 5:00 p.m. 12 Sep 2026      All 41,000 only; no financing condition. Settlement\n  bid                          1% seller fee: $2,890.50                   Settles 20 Sep 2026                precedes 15 Oct deadline; expected net exceeds\n                               Expected net: $286,159.50                                                     $280,000 by $6,159.50.\n\n  Sponsor tender               Full-acceptance scenario only:             Tender closes 5:00 p.m. 30 Sep     Any number may be tendered, but 5,000,000-unit\n                               Gross $311,600                             2026                               program limit may cause proration. Acceptance and\n                               2% fee: $6,232                             Expected settlement 15 Nov 2026    accepted count are not guaranteed. Expected\n                               Net $305,368                                                                  settlement follows deadline.\n\n  Hold                         No stated sale proceeds or fee             No scheduled liquidity before 15   Ordinary redemption remains suspended; no\n                                                                          Oct 2026                           announced resumption. Does not fund the firm\n                                                                                                             deposit on the record.\n\n\n The tender’s potential full-acceptance premium is $22,550 gross ($311,600 versus $289,050) or $19,208.50 net ($305,368 versus $286,159.50). It is nominally higher but cannot satisfy the firm\n liquidity deadline: expected settlement is after 15 October and proration can reduce accepted units. The withdrawal right through the deadline does not create liquidity, and no accelerated settlement\n has been offered.\n\n Requirement collision\n Clara’s wish not to sell excess units is reasonable. So is the need for cleared proceeds by the firm date. They conflict because the only documented bid that clears in time is all-or-none for 41,000\n units; the buyer will not purchase a partial position. The documented sale produces $6,159.50 more cash than required, but it is the bounded trade-off that secures the deadline without inventing\n another buyer.\n The current bid is the only documented current secondary bid: $7.05/unit for all 41,000 units, no financing condition, 1% seller fee, expiry at 5:00 p.m. on 12 September 2026, and settlement on\n 20 September 2026.\n\n\n\n\n                                                                                                                                                                                Clara Bruns | Legal review | 3\n\fWILLOWMERE SOLUTIONS / CONFIDENTIAL CLIENT CASE RESPONSE\n\n\n\n LIMITS AND DISPOSITION\n\n\n                                                 What the record supports — and does not\n This is a bounded liquidity decision, not a formal appraisal, tax opinion, or forecast.\n\n Material limits\n  Known from the record                                                                   Not determined by the record\n\n  The secondary bid is executable on its stated all-unit terms and has no financing       Client-specific tax consequences; future unit value; future distributions; sponsor\n  condition. The tender is conditional, subject to a 5,000,000-unit limit, and expected   redemption resumption; tender acceptance or proration; and any future secondary\n  to settle after the funding date. Historical trades were completed but smaller than     price or buyer. No value is assigned to these unknowns.\n  Clara’s position.\n\n Basis and tax context\n Against the recorded aggregate cost basis of $502,250, the documented sale’s expected net proceeds of $286,159.50 are an economic/cash difference of $216,090.50. That comparison is not an\n exact deductible loss, tax result, or tax benefit. Tax treatment depends on client-specific information not in this closed record.\n\n Decision rationale\n The stated NAV remains useful as a dated appraisal-based reference, but it does not cure the funding constraint or override realizable evidence. Holding has no scheduled liquidity before the\n deposit date. The tender’s higher full-acceptance net scenario is conditional and expected after the deadline. The documented secondary bid is the only listed alternative that has an identified buyer,\n a stated settlement before the deadline, and expected net proceeds sufficient for the deposit.\n\n\n                            DECISION\n\n\n                            Decline the sponsor tender and accept the documented all-or-none secondary bid for all 41,000 units at\n                            $7.05 per unit, producing expected net proceeds of $286,159.50 and cleared funds on 20 September\n                            2026—before the firm 15 October 2026 deposit deadline.\n\n\n\n\n                                                                                                                                                                                Clara Bruns | Legal review | 4\n\f","truncated":false,"note":"PDF 文字提取；可打开原文件查看排版。"}}],"rubric":[{"rubric_item_id":"R01","check":"file_introspection","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The required deliverable exists as a readable, non-corrupt PDF named exactly clara_bruns_reit_decision.pdf.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":true,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Submitted file and PDF metadata","grader_hint":"Confirm the exact filename, PDF type, successful opening, and extractable or visibly rendered content."},{"rubric_item_id":"R02","check":"geometry","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The complete response is no more than four rendered pages.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Rendered PDF page count","grader_hint":"Count all PDF pages, including cover or appendix pages if present."},{"rubric_item_id":"R03","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The response correctly identifies Clara Bruns, her 41,000-unit position, and Tanneryside Trading Company.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Case identification in the PDF","grader_hint":"All three identifiers must appear and must not conflict elsewhere in the response."},{"rubric_item_id":"R04","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The document is framed as a decision-ready client case response for legal review, not merely as a generic investment summary.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Opening, decision framing, and conclusion","grader_hint":"Look for a clear client objective, alternatives, governing constraints, analysis, and disposition."},{"rubric_item_id":"R05","check":"value_tolerance","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The recorded aggregate cost basis is stated as $502,250 with a tolerance of ±$1.","weight":1.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Position or tax-context discussion","grader_hint":"Accept $12.25 per unit if it is also correctly reconciled to $502,250 within ±$1."},{"rubric_item_id":"R06","check":"value_tolerance","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The sponsor-stated NAV is reported as $10.42 per unit and $427,220 in aggregate, each within ±$0.01 per unit and ±$1 in aggregate.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Valuation evidence section","grader_hint":"Both the per-unit figure and its correctly extended position value must appear."},{"rubric_item_id":"R07","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The response associates the $10.42 NAV with the 31 December 2023 appraisal date, the 28 March 2024 posting date, and the absence of a later refresh.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"NAV provenance and limitations","grader_hint":"All three timing facts must be linked to the NAV rather than presented as unrelated dates."},{"rubric_item_id":"R08","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The response actively discourages treating the stale sponsor NAV as a current cash-equivalent, guaranteed sale price, or directly realizable market value.","weight":4.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"NAV interpretation and client-facing language","grader_hint":"Credit requires an affirmative correction in clear language, not merely labeling NAV as an estimate."},{"rubric_item_id":"R09","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The explanation distinguishes appraisal-based NAV from evidence generated by actual transactions and executable offers without claiming that either is a perfectly observable public-market price.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Valuation-method discussion","grader_hint":"Look for a balanced distinction appropriate to an illiquid non-traded investment."},{"rubric_item_id":"R10","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The valuation range has identifiable lower and upper anchors drawn from documented realizable alternatives, and it does not mechanically average the $10.42 NAV with secondary evidence.","weight":4.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Stated valuation range and supporting explanation","grader_hint":"A strong answer uses the $7.05 executable secondary bid and $7.60 conditional sponsor tender as the present gross realizability bounds, while clearly qualifying the upper bound. Accept another tightly reasoned range only if every endpoint is documented and its limitations are explicit."},{"rubric_item_id":"R11","check":"value_tolerance","category":"deterministic","criterion":"If the present gross realizability range is expressed using the executable bid and sponsor tender, its per-unit endpoints are $7.05 and $7.60 within ±$0.01.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Valuation range","grader_hint":"Apply this item when the response presents the preferred present-evidence range; endpoints must not be reversed or unlabeled."},{"rubric_item_id":"R12","check":"value_tolerance","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The gross position-value equivalents of $7.05 and $7.60 are shown as $289,050 and $311,600, respectively, each within ±$1.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Valuation range or alternative comparison","grader_hint":"Verify each amount equals the stated per-unit figure multiplied by 41,000 units."},{"rubric_item_id":"R13","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The response identifies the four expired mini-tenders with their respective prices, capacity limits, and expiry dates and clearly classifies all four as expired rather than currently executable.","weight":4.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Historical bid coverage","grader_hint":"Check the complete pairs: $6.35/25,000/31 Jan 2025; $6.70/60,000/31 Mar 2025; $7.15/35,000/30 Jun 2025; $7.40/50,000/31 Aug 2025."},{"rubric_item_id":"R14","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"Expired mini-tenders are used as historical indications of changing bidder appetite, not as proof that Clara sold units or can still obtain those prices.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Interpretation of historical bids","grader_hint":"The document must preserve the distinction between an unsolicited offer and a completed transaction."},{"rubric_item_id":"R15","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The response covers all nine completed secondary transactions with the correct quarter, unit quantity, and clearing price.","weight":5.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Historical transaction analysis","grader_hint":"Verify Q4 2024: 4,000 at $6.20 and 2,500 at $6.35; Q1 2025: 5,000 at $6.55 and 3,000 at $6.70; Q2 2025: 2,000 at $6.85, 7,500 at $7.00, and 4,000 at $7.10; Q3 2025: 1,500 at $7.20 and 6,000 at $7.05."},{"rubric_item_id":"R16","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The response clearly distinguishes completed secondary transactions from listings, bids, and sponsor-reported NAV.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Evidence labels and narrative","grader_hint":"A reader should be able to tell which observations document an actual clearing event."},{"rubric_item_id":"R17","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The period comparison explains the material upward movement from late 2024 through 2025 while acknowledging dispersion and the small, irregular transaction samples.","weight":4.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Period-over-period analysis","grader_hint":"Credit requires both the directional conclusion and a limitation on overinterpreting sparse illiquid-market observations."},{"rubric_item_id":"R18","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The document compares at least two distinct periods using like-for-like secondary clearing evidence rather than treating an appraisal, an expired offer, and a completed trade as interchangeable observations.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Period comparison","grader_hint":"Confirm that the comparison basis is explicit and consistent."},{"rubric_item_id":"R19","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The response states that the ordinary redemption plan was suspended on 14 November 2024 and has not been announced as resumed.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Liquidity analysis","grader_hint":"Both the suspension date and no-resumption status must be present."},{"rubric_item_id":"R20","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The hold alternative is assessed as providing no scheduled liquidity before 15 October 2026 without asserting that the units are permanently unsaleable or worthless.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Hold-alternative analysis","grader_hint":"Look for a time-bounded liquidity conclusion and appropriately restrained wording."},{"rubric_item_id":"R21","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The sponsor tender is accurately described as $7.60 per unit, open until 30 September 2026, subject to a 2% fee, and expected to settle on 15 November 2026.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Tender-alternative analysis","grader_hint":"All four terms must be associated with the sponsor tender."},{"rubric_item_id":"R22","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The response identifies the 5,000,000-unit program cap and explains that oversubscription could cause proration, leaving acceptance and accepted unit count uncertain.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Tender risk discussion","grader_hint":"Credit requires the practical consequence, not just repetition of the cap."},{"rubric_item_id":"R23","check":"value_tolerance","category":"deterministic","criterion":"Full acceptance of all 41,000 units in the sponsor tender is calculated as $311,600 gross and $305,368 net after the 2% fee, each within ±$1.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Tender economics","grader_hint":"The amounts must be labeled as a full-acceptance scenario rather than guaranteed proceeds."},{"rubric_item_id":"R24","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The response does not rely on the tender withdrawal right or any undocumented accelerated settlement as a solution to Clara’s funding deadline.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Tender timing analysis","grader_hint":"The document may mention withdrawal through the deadline but must not imply that withdrawal creates liquidity."},{"rubric_item_id":"R25","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The current secondary bid is accurately described as $7.05 per unit for all 41,000 units, expiring 12 September 2026, settling 20 September 2026, carrying a 1% seller fee, and having no financing condition.","weight":4.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Secondary-sale alternative","grader_hint":"All material bid terms must be included and associated with the same offer."},{"rubric_item_id":"R26","check":"value_tolerance","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The current secondary sale is calculated as $289,050 gross within ±$1.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Secondary-sale economics","grader_hint":"Verify $7.05 multiplied by 41,000 units."},{"rubric_item_id":"R27","check":"value_tolerance","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The current secondary sale is calculated as $286,159.50 net after the 1% seller fee, within ±$1.","weight":4.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Secondary-sale economics and recommendation","grader_hint":"This is the expected net-proceeds anchor for the recommended secondary sale."},{"rubric_item_id":"R28","check":"value_tolerance","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The response shows that the documented secondary sale exceeds Clara’s $280,000 cash requirement by $6,159.50 within ±$1.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Funding sufficiency analysis","grader_hint":"Verify net secondary proceeds less the required cash amount."},{"rubric_item_id":"R29","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The response compares each alternative’s settlement timing directly with the 15 October 2026 cleared-funds deadline.","weight":4.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Alternative comparison","grader_hint":"The secondary bid clears before the deadline; the tender is expected to settle after it; holding has no scheduled liquidity by it."},{"rubric_item_id":"R30","check":"value_tolerance","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The potential full-acceptance tender premium over the secondary sale is stated as $22,550 gross or $19,208.50 net, with tolerance of ±$1 for whichever comparison is used.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Trade-off analysis","grader_hint":"The comparison must use like-for-like gross or net figures and identify the tender amount as conditional."},{"rubric_item_id":"R31","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The document explains that the nominally higher tender proceeds do not satisfy the firm liquidity deadline because settlement follows the deadline and acceptance may be prorated.","weight":5.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Core decision reasoning","grader_hint":"This must be a causal explanation, not merely a table containing dates."},{"rubric_item_id":"R32","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The response explicitly recognizes the collision between Clara’s desire not to sell excess units and the secondary buyer’s all-or-none condition.","weight":4.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Client-requirement trade-off","grader_hint":"Both individually reasonable requirements and their incompatibility must be stated."},{"rubric_item_id":"R33","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The response resolves the all-or-none trade-off by prioritizing the hard cleared-funds deadline and quantifies the excess cash rather than obscuring it.","weight":5.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Decision rationale","grader_hint":"Look for an explicit bounded choice supported by the $6,159.50 excess-proceeds calculation."},{"rubric_item_id":"R34","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The response makes exactly one unambiguous recommendation among tendering, holding, and accepting the documented secondary bid.","weight":5.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Recommendation language","grader_hint":"Conditional branches, multiple co-equal recommendations, or leaving the choice to Clara without a professional call do not satisfy this item."},{"rubric_item_id":"R35","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The single recommendation is to decline or not pursue the sponsor tender and accept the documented all-unit secondary bid.","weight":6.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Final recommendation","grader_hint":"Equivalent unequivocal wording is acceptable. The recommendation must address both the tender question and the selected alternative."},{"rubric_item_id":"R36","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"Every occurrence of the recommended expected net proceeds agrees with $286,159.50 within ±$1.","weight":4.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Executive summary, comparison, and conclusion","grader_hint":"Search the entire PDF for net-proceeds figures associated with the recommended sale and confirm consistency."},{"rubric_item_id":"R37","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The recommendation does not depend on an undocumented buyer, a partial purchase the current buyer has refused, or guaranteed full tender acceptance.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Recommendation assumptions","grader_hint":"The selected path must stay within the documented feasible set."},{"rubric_item_id":"R38","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The document distinguishes economic loss from tax treatment and does not present the difference from recorded basis as an exact deductible loss or exact tax benefit.","weight":4.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Basis and tax discussion","grader_hint":"Credit requires appropriate caution that tax result depends on information not in the record."},{"rubric_item_id":"R39","check":"value_tolerance","category":"deterministic","criterion":"If the economic difference between cost basis and recommended net proceeds is quantified, it equals $216,090.50 within ±$1 and is labeled as an economic or cash difference rather than an exact tax result.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Basis comparison","grader_hint":"Verify $502,250 minus $286,159.50 and inspect the accompanying label."},{"rubric_item_id":"R40","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"Material unknowns are identified without invented values, including the client-specific tax consequences and any future value, distribution, redemption resumption, or tender proration outcome that affects the discussion.","weight":4.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Limitations and caveats","grader_hint":"The response need not list every conceivable unknown, but it must flag those material to interpreting the recommendation."},{"rubric_item_id":"R41","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The response does not imply that historical price improvement guarantees continued appreciation or that the $7.20 historical clearing price is currently available for all 41,000 units.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Historical-market interpretation","grader_hint":"Look for restrained, non-promissory treatment of the trend and transaction scale."},{"rubric_item_id":"R42","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The response explains the large NAV-to-secondary gap using defensible characteristics of the evidence—staleness, appraisal basis, illiquidity, transaction scale, fees, and execution conditions—without simply splitting the difference.","weight":5.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Reconciliation of valuation evidence","grader_hint":"Credit requires reasoned reconciliation. A midpoint presented without an evidence-based rationale is insufficient."},{"rubric_item_id":"R43","check":"value_tolerance","category":"deterministic","criterion":"If the recommended $7.05 bid is compared with the $10.42 NAV, the response calculates either a 32.34% discount to NAV or a 47.80% NAV premium over the bid, with tolerance of ±0.2 percentage points and clear denominator labeling.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"NAV reconciliation","grader_hint":"Accept either formulation only when the direction and denominator are explicit."},{"rubric_item_id":"R44","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The response gives appropriate weight to transaction size when comparing small historical prints with a bid covering Clara’s entire 41,000-unit position.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Market-evidence assessment","grader_hint":"Look for recognition that small-lot clearing prices do not automatically establish execution value for the full position."},{"rubric_item_id":"R45","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The alternative comparison uses consistent terminology for gross proceeds, fees, net proceeds, expiry, settlement, and conditionality.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Figures and labels throughout the PDF","grader_hint":"Check that figures are not mixed across gross and net bases and that bid expiry is not confused with settlement."},{"rubric_item_id":"R46","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The final substantive statement of the document is the decision, not an open-ended next-steps list, a request for further analysis, or a transfer of the recommendation back to legal counsel.","weight":4.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Closing lines of the PDF","grader_hint":"Inspect the final paragraph or final presented element and confirm that it closes unequivocally on the recommendation."},{"rubric_item_id":"R47","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The response is usable in a client conversation: concise, plain-language, visually legible, and candid about trade-offs without overwhelming Clara with raw records.","weight":4.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Rendered document as a whole","grader_hint":"Assess whether a financial advisor could present the response directly without rewriting or orally repairing ambiguous conclusions."},{"rubric_item_id":"R48","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The response is suitable for legal review because factual assertions are traceable to the closed record, limitations are conspicuous, and the recommendation does not overstate certainty or the advisor’s authority.","weight":4.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Rendered document as a whole","grader_hint":"Evaluate internal traceability, qualification of conditional outcomes, and consistency with a bounded advisory decision."}],"spec":{"professional_context":"Willowmere Solutions is documenting a bounded liquidity decision for an older client holding an illiquid non-traded real estate investment. Legal counsel needs a numerically consistent response that distinguishes appraisal-based NAV from realizable evidence and resolves a hard funding deadline.","difficulty_sources":["Reconciling stale appraisal-based NAV with completed trades and executable but conditional offers","Resolving a higher-price tender against a non-negotiable liquidity deadline","Resolving the client’s minimum-sale preference against an all-or-none executable bid","Maintaining complete historical bid and transaction coverage within a four-page decision response","Separating economic loss from unsupported tax conclusions"],"authoring_plan":{"mode":"decision","solver_instance":"A personal financial advisor must turn a closed factual record concerning an illiquid non-traded real estate investment into a legally reviewable client decision response.","intended_verdict":"Recommend that Clara Bruns not tender and instead accept the documented all-or-none secondary bid for all 41,000 units at $7.05 per unit, because its expected $286,159.50 net proceeds settle before the firm 15 October 2026 funding deadline. Explicitly resolve the fact that this produces $6,159.50 more cash than Clara needs and sells more units than her minimum-cash preference would otherwise require.","planted_tensions":["The sponsor tender offers a higher nominal price and higher hypothetical net proceeds, but expected settlement occurs after the client’s firm cleared-funds deadline and acceptance can be prorated.","The client reasonably wants no more units sold than necessary, but the only documented secondary buyer that can satisfy the deadline will purchase only the complete 41,000-unit position.","Sponsor NAV is prominently stated and much higher than transaction evidence, but it rests on an old appraisal and is not a realizable cash price."],"common_misuse_to_correct":"Do not describe the $10.42 sponsor NAV or $427,220 extension as what the client can currently realize. The gold should actively explain why appraisal-based NAV differs from executable and completed-market evidence.","gold_relationships":{"cost_basis":"41,000 units × $12.25 recorded basis per unit = $502,250.","nav_extension":"41,000 units × $10.42 = $427,220.","secondary_gross":"41,000 units × $7.05 = $289,050.","secondary_fee":"$289,050 × 1% = $2,890.50.","secondary_net":"$289,050 − $2,890.50 = $286,159.50.","secondary_cash_excess":"$286,159.50 − $280,000 required cash = $6,159.50.","tender_gross_full_acceptance":"41,000 units × $7.60 = $311,600.","tender_fee_full_acceptance":"$311,600 × 2% = $6,232.","tender_net_full_acceptance":"$311,600 − $6,232 = $305,368.","gross_tender_premium":"$311,600 − $289,050 = $22,550.","net_tender_premium":"$305,368 − $286,159.50 = $19,208.50.","economic_basis_difference":"$502,250 − $286,159.50 = $216,090.50; this is not an exact tax deduction.","discount_to_nav":"($10.42 − $7.05) ÷ $10.42 = 32.34%.","nav_premium_over_bid":"($10.42 − $7.05) ÷ $7.05 = 47.80%.","timing_test":"20 September 2026 secondary settlement precedes 15 October 2026; 15 November 2026 expected tender settlement follows it; holding has no scheduled liquidity before it.","preferred_realizability_range":"$7.05 to $7.60 per unit gross, or $289,050 to $311,600 gross for the position, with the upper endpoint expressly labeled conditional and untimely for the client’s need. The corresponding documented net outcomes are $286,159.50 and $305,368 under full tender acceptance.","period_change":"Completed secondary clearing prices generally rose from $6.20–$6.35 in Q4 2024 to $7.05–$7.20 in Q3 2025, but observations are sparse, transaction sizes vary, and none establishes a guaranteed current full-position price."},"coverage_requirements":["Carry every expired mini-tender price, capacity, and expiry accurately and classify each as historical rather than executable.","Carry all nine completed secondary transactions accurately by quarter, quantity, and clearing price.","Distinguish sponsor NAV, unsolicited mini-tenders, completed secondary trades, the current executable secondary bid, and the current conditional sponsor tender.","State the redemption suspension date and no announced resumption.","Compare tender, hold, and secondary sale on proceeds, fees, execution certainty, and timing.","Identify material unknowns without assigning values, especially client-specific tax treatment and future sponsor or market outcomes."],"must_not_assert":["The sponsor NAV is current market value, cash-equivalent value, or a guaranteed sale price.","The sponsor tender will accept all 41,000 units or settle before the stated expected date.","An expired mini-tender remains available or proves that Clara completed a transaction.","A small historical secondary trade guarantees execution for the full position.","The economic difference from basis is an exact deductible tax loss or produces a known tax benefit.","Historical price improvement guarantees continued appreciation.","An undocumented buyer, partial purchase, accelerated tender settlement, future distribution, or redemption-plan resumption exists."],"space_feasibility":"The required work fits within four pages at normal professional presentation density: one concise decision summary, a compact evidence and period comparison, an alternatives table or equivalent, a short risk and limitation discussion, and a closing recommendation. The response need not reproduce raw records in prose if compact presentation preserves every graded fact.","offline_validation":"All required facts are contained in the request. Every arithmetic relationship can be recalculated with a calculator or Python, and the PDF can be authored and rendered using standard office libraries without internet access."},"design_constraints":{"artifact_scope":"mixed_media_bundle","structure":"strict_schema"},"reference_files":[],"deliverables":[{"deliverable_id":"client_case_response","filename":"clara_bruns_reit_decision.pdf","media_type":"application/pdf","description":"A client case response presenting the valuation range, period comparison, alternative economics, and one tender-or-sale decision for legal review.","required":true,"constraints":["Use only the closed-record facts in the request; no more than four pages; include exactly one recommendation with a consistent net-proceeds anchor; finish on the decision; do not invent figures or state an exact tax result."],"rendering_required":true}],"work_activity":"make_decisions","work_product":"customer_case_response","internet_policy":"forbidden","evidence_policy":"professional_judgment"},"source_metadata":{"prompt_version":"authored_doc@v4","occupation":"personal_financial_advisors","sector":"finance_insurance","task_archetype":"authored_doc","estimated_professional_hours":3.5,"gold_policy_self_report_not_verified":{"author_type":"domain_expert","reviewer_policy":"The gold response must be independently reviewed by a personal financial planning professional and a legal or compliance reviewer familiar with illiquid investment communications. Reviewers should recalculate all proceeds, test the recommendation against the hard liquidity deadline, and inspect the rendered PDF for consistency and overstatement.","minimum_reviewers":2,"known_limitations":["The case uses a closed fictional record and does not determine actual tax treatment, future distributions, future secondary prices, tender participation, or sponsor solvency. The valuation range is a decision-oriented realizability range rather than a formal appraisal."],"reference_solution_passed_rubric":true}},"validation":{"label":"结构/材料验收记录","detail":"validated 属于结构、防泄漏、材料推进验收；没有内容正确率结论。","formal":false,"release_ready_recorded":true,"checks":[{"check":"package_integrity","validator":"gdpval-package","validator_version":"v1","passed":true,"required":true,"metrics":{},"evidence_artifact_ids":[],"detail":"","checked_at":"2026-08-24T13:25:43+00:00"},{"check":"gold_isolation","validator":"gdpval-package","validator_version":"v1","passed":true,"required":true,"metrics":{},"evidence_artifact_ids":[],"detail":"","checked_at":"2026-08-24T13:25:43+00:00"},{"check":"artifact_format","validator":"gdpval-package","validator_version":"v1","passed":true,"required":true,"metrics":{},"evidence_artifact_ids":[],"detail":"","checked_at":"2026-08-24T13:25:43+00:00"},{"check":"rendering","validator":"gdpval-package","validator_version":"v1","passed":true,"required":true,"metrics":{},"evidence_artifact_ids":[],"detail":"","checked_at":"2026-08-24T13:25:43+00:00"},{"check":"instruction_rubric_overlap","validator":"gdpval-package","validator_version":"v1","passed":false,"required":false,"metrics":{},"evidence_artifact_ids":[],"detail":"instruction 与 rubric criterion 存在 8-gram 逐字重叠: ['R25']","checked_at":"2026-08-24T13:25:43+00:00"}]},"source_path":"/inspire/hdd2/project/special-project-1/public/ynan/gdpval_data/corpus_c1/runs/15968b8295d97f81","declared_deliverables":["clara_bruns_reit_decision.pdf"]};
